    "text": "Let <math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> and <math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> be the respective electric fields at a distance r from a point charge Q, an infinitely long wire with constant linear charge density <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#955;</mi></math> , and an infinite plane with uniform surface charge density <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#963;</mi></math> . If <math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mfenced></math> at a given distance <math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></math> , then",
